WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:02.358
[helmet clanging]

00:00:02.358 --> 00:00:03.725
[knight groaning]

00:00:03.725 --> 00:00:04.750
[armor clanging on ground]

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.728
Toby Capwell: Poor old R-Patz

00:00:06.728 --> 00:00:09.590
looks like a pile of garbage, I'm afraid.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.680
Hi, I'm Toby Capwell.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.160
I'm curator of arms and armor
at The Wallace Collection

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.210
in Central London,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.790
but I'm also a practitioner.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.150
Since I was a kid, I've been riding horses

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.000
and fighting in armor.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.070
I've taken part in competitions,

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.970
armored combat all over the world.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.970
And today we're going to
be looking at the treatment

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.053
of medieval arms and armor in cinema.

00:00:32.444 --> 00:00:34.585
[punching]

00:00:36.227 --> 00:00:37.290
[laser gunfire]
[armor clanging]

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.380
Oh, stop! Stop.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:39.830
Did you see that?

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.360
He just got shot in the
armor, and it knocked him,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.140
but it didn't stop him.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.240
And in a complete armor,

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.000
a medieval knight can suffer
horrendous physical punishment

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.030
that would kill an unarmored
person [snaps] like that,

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.800
but you can take the hits.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.075
And in armored combat,

00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:01.450
there are times when you decide

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.210
to let your opponent hit you

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.070
because you know you can take it

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.360
and you're gonna do something else.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.770
You're not gonna waste time defending

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.670
what doesn't need to be defended.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.520
And the armor in this
clip takes on, again,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.070
narrative significance. It's storytelling.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.040
He can take a shot right in the chest,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.030
so that's a pretty big character point,

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.020
wouldn't you think?

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.700
He's not clunking around, all cumbersome.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.750
He moves like a real knight.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.210
I've been jousting and
fighting in armor for 30 years.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.250
I've built a lot of
different kinds of armor,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.650
working with different
craftsmen all over the world.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.110
Real armor of the late Middle Ages,

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.980
the Renaissance, is like a steel skin.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.250
It's not bulky.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.503
It's your shape.

00:01:45.503 --> 00:01:47.840
The armor should fit you like a skin.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.358
It should follow your musculature.

00:01:50.358 --> 00:01:52.650
It doesn't have to be a
lot of space under there.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.250
It's fitted. It moves with you.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.050
The plates are hardened and
tempered medium-carbon steel,

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.890
so they don't have to be thick and chunky.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.782
They need to glide over each other

00:02:03.782 --> 00:02:06.015
and move like you move.

00:02:06.015 --> 00:02:08.240
Mando: Tell me where the Mandalorians are,

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.560
and I'll walk out of
here without killing you.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.365
Toby: He hardly ever takes his helmet off,

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.500
and yet you know everything
about his expression,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.315
his feelings, his character.

00:02:16.315 --> 00:02:20.810
Historically, a knight has a very close,

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.190
personal relationship with his armor.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.530
The armor is responsible for his safety,

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.900
but he's also the guy who's
gonna mold your character.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.900
That's never really been
explored until this.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.560
Stormtrooper: I said, where are they?

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.755
Toby: It's got a couple of possible

00:02:40.755 --> 00:02:42.930
sources of inspiration. On the one hand,

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.688
it looks a lot like

00:02:44.688 --> 00:02:48.170
an ancient Greek hoplite's helmet,

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.550
the ancient Greek heavy infantry of, like,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.640
500 BC or of thereabouts.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.310
These very distinct spectacled helmets

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.460
with these eye cutouts,

00:02:58.460 --> 00:02:59.969
they have a nasal for the nose

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:01.810
and then the cheeks brought in.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.450
In the Bronze Age, they're trying, again,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.730
to get as much protection for the face

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.770
and the head as they possibly can.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.155
So that's where that really closely set

00:03:11.155 --> 00:03:15.440
kind of spectacled profile comes from.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.520
That was then imitated in the Renaissance.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.510
In the 15th century in Italy,

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.840
and very rapidly everywhere else,

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.600
people became fascinated with
the classical world again,

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.990
and they started looking
back to the ancient Greek

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.280
and Roman precedent.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.460
And this style of helmet
became fashionable again

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.090
in Italy and Venice,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.030
in the Venetian empire, specifically.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:40.600
In the 15th century,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.010
most used by infantry,

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.012
shooting crossbows and firearms and...

00:03:46.012 --> 00:03:48.103
more lightly armored troops.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.350
And in the ancient Greek period,

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.450
they were the heavy infantry,

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.517
but they still had shields to hide behind,

00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:57.648
and they're actively
protecting their faces

00:03:57.648 --> 00:04:01.270
as well as relying on their armor.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.461
I'd give this a 10, absolutely.

00:04:04.529 --> 00:04:05.807
[knight grunting]

00:04:05.807 --> 00:04:07.910
"Vikings" is a fantasy.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.450
You should really treat it as a fantasy.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.850
There's a lot of things
to like about this show.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.470
And that makes it almost
the more tragic for me,

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.690
because those Anglo-Saxons

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.370
are all wearing late-16th-century helmets.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.623
The burgonets, with the
high comb and the peak.

00:04:25.623 --> 00:04:29.527
They are 800 years wrong.

00:04:29.527 --> 00:04:30.360
[armor clanging]

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.470
[man screaming in pain]

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.923
The leather biker gear.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.340
There is almost no historical evidence

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.920
for leather clothing.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.486
Almost across the board,

00:04:41.486 --> 00:04:45.310
people in the past did not
make clothes out of leather.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:48.400
It's a distinctly modern thing.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.233
[horse neighing]

00:04:49.233 --> 00:04:52.610
[man shouting]

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:53.570
The ax is fine.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.090
I don't have a problem with the ax.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.838
I do have a problem with

00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:59.130
none of the main
characters wearing helmets.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:02.880
You can't go anywhere
near a battle like this

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:03.860
without a helmet.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.610
So, I mean, someone like Ragnar Lodbrok

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.359
or someone of that stature

00:05:07.359 --> 00:05:09.240
would have an amazing mail coat.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.410
He'd have jewelry.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.693
He'd have big, thick, gold stuff on him.

00:05:13.693 --> 00:05:15.230
He'd have a gilded sword.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:19.490
He does not have to look
like a pile of leather crap.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.532
I'm gonna give it a three.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.773
[dramatic music]

00:05:26.773 --> 00:05:28.350
[armor clanging]

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.280
That is metal.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.780
Nothing else looks like metal

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.130
but metal in close-up.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.970
All that decoration is etched
into the steel with acid.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.552
This is film armor as good as it gets.

00:05:42.552 --> 00:05:44.860
[weapons clanging]
[dramatic music]

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.190
Now, there are lots of
great spraying techniques,

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.550
and you can spray plastic
to look reflective.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.270
And increasingly it looks
more and more like metal,

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.460
but it doesn't behave like metal.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.943
Their armor, you can see,

00:05:55.943 --> 00:05:58.242
it's a little bit too flexy.

00:05:58.242 --> 00:06:00.888
There's something about metal that,

00:06:00.888 --> 00:06:04.021
it has a weight, it has a rigidity

00:06:04.021 --> 00:06:05.445
that rubber and plastic

00:06:05.445 --> 00:06:07.310
and polyurethane don't have.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:08.143
[swishing mace]

00:06:08.143 --> 00:06:09.135
[men shouting]

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.500
[swishing mace]

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.858
He walks into the battle,

00:06:12.858 --> 00:06:15.133
and you know everything you
need to know about this guy,

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.320
and it's all coming out through his gear.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:21.580
They've taken real
15th-century armor styles,

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.000
mostly German, and then
turned it up to 11.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.810
Sauron is wielding a mace.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:28.967
And maces are real.

00:06:28.967 --> 00:06:32.780
It's a hafted weapon with
a wooden or a steel haft

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.800
with a flanged or bladed head on one end.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.130
So all the weight of the
weapon is balanced to the end.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.770
And it's a concussion weapon.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.030
And you're trying, rather than to cut

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:45.250
or pierce your opponent,

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.900
you're trying to smash
through their defenses.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:51.813
That is way bigger in proportion to him

00:06:51.813 --> 00:06:54.270
than any real maces were.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:58.620
Real German Gothic high medieval maces

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.670
of the 15th century are,

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.330
they're usually pretty short.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.200
They're just a little bit more than a foot

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.620
or a bit more long.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.510
The heads are quite small,

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:12.100
but they got these really
sharp blades on them,

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:16.410
and you're focusing your
energy on a really small area.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.867
A weapon doesn't need to be massive

00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:22.640
to have the effect that it needs to have.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.080
But massive always reads well on screen,

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:28.660
especially when you've
got a dark lord of Mordor.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.990
And how successfully does
it use armor and weapons

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:35.550
to bring its characters
and its themes across?

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.717
I gotta give this 10 as well.

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:39.958
[armor clanging]

00:07:39.958 --> 00:07:41.890
Poor old R-Patz

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:44.460
looks like a pile of garbage, I'm afraid.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.210
This is a mishmash.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.700
There are a couple of elements,

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.700
the shoulders and the body armor,

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.420
that are vaguely 15th century,

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.370
but they're about a generation
too late for Agincourt.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.090
His helmet is just ridiculous.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:02.900
I mean, that has a vaguely
16th-century profile to it,

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.340
but it fits badly.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.755
It's too big. It's clunky.

00:08:06.755 --> 00:08:08.200
[knight groaning]

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.750
[armor clanging on ground]

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.750
Ah, come on, man. What is this?

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.470
[armor clanging]

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.415
Well, this is obviously

00:08:16.415 --> 00:08:20.690
supposed to be the battle
of Agincourt in 1415.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.380
[sighs]

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:23.380
[clicks tongue]

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:26.500
[producer laughing]

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:29.670
At the real battle of
Agincourt, it was very muddy.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.490
The French had 10,000 fully armed knights.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.290
That was the entirety of their army.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.270
They had archers, but
they didn't use them.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:40.440
The Pas-de-Calais mud is like
this thick, gluey clay stuff,

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:42.580
very difficult to move through.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:43.610
And it would be difficult,

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:45.400
regardless of what you're wearing.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.130
Armor often gets blamed for
the French defeat at Agincourt,

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.061
as if the French were wearing heavy armor

00:08:51.061 --> 00:08:54.620
that the English were too
clever to wear or something.

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:58.345
The fact is, the English,
including Henry V here,

00:08:58.345 --> 00:09:01.130
were wearing full-plate armor of the time.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:04.250
The English had 1,500 fully armed knights

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.245
whose job it was to protect
those famous archers.

00:09:07.245 --> 00:09:09.376
[men shouting]

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:16.700
They go about it sort of the right way,

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.720
in that this knight is
someone who's been trained

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:22.915
to fight and kill people
since he was a child,

00:09:22.915 --> 00:09:25.070
and he's very, very good at it.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:27.343
The only way you've got any chance of this

00:09:27.343 --> 00:09:28.250
if you're an archer

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.070
or an English foot soldier or whatever

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.960
is to get six or seven
or eight of your friends

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.070
and pile in all at the same time.

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.554
Don't take turns. He will kill all of you.

00:09:38.554 --> 00:09:40.680
You gotta all go together,

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.380
and four or five of you
gotta hold him down,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.260
and then one or two of you
have to stab him in the eyes

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.930
and in the gaps in the armor.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:49.770
You don't stab through the armor.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.978
When King Richard III was killed

00:09:51.978 --> 00:09:53.040
at the battle of Bosworth,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.482
they immobilized him. They held him down.

00:09:55.482 --> 00:09:56.830
Someone took his helmet off.

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:59.810
They probably cut the chin
strap and took the helmet off,

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.030
and then they can do as they like.

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:02.830
They cut off the back of his skull.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.730
They stabbed him right through the brain.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.370
They did whatever they wanted.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.750
I'll give it a one, 'cause they've got mud

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.810
and archers working together.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.471
But I can't give it any more than a one.

00:10:13.471 --> 00:10:16.689
It's a massive lost opportunity.

00:10:16.689 --> 00:10:18.049
[crowd cheering]

00:10:18.049 --> 00:10:19.750
[horses running]

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.920
It's a legal procedure

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.220
where they're determining
the ownership of territory

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.620
with a trial by combat.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:29.810
They're attacking each
other on the wrong side.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:30.643
If you can see,

00:10:30.643 --> 00:10:33.223
they're passing right to
right, which is wrong.

00:10:33.223 --> 00:10:34.590
They shouldn't be doing that.

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.510
Because your reign hand
is in your left hand,

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:40.110
so it can't wield a weapon.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.220
Your reign hand is in your left,

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.070
so your left side is more exposed.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:44.840
So that's where your shield goes,

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.033
and your lance is in your right hand.

00:10:46.033 --> 00:10:48.370
Nobody's allowed to be
left-handed in the Middle Ages.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:51.800
In traditional knightly
mounted combat like this,

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.720
you always pass on the
left side to left side.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.097
Unhorsing someone is very difficult,

00:10:58.097 --> 00:11:00.980
and it only happens
when certain key factors

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.640
all come together.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.470
The timing's got to be right.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:07.100
The impact, the placement of the lance

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.130
on the body's got to be right.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.680
Where he is in his stride of
the horse when he gets hit.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.040
There's lots of things
that kind of contribute

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:17.440
to the likelihood of being unhorsed.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:19.080
But as a general rule,

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.338
it's very, very difficult

00:11:22.338 --> 00:11:25.594
to separate a good
horseman from his horse.

00:11:25.594 --> 00:11:27.989
[horse running]

00:11:27.989 --> 00:11:29.750
[horse neighing]

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.440
If this was a later period,

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:34.215
in, like, the 15th century

00:11:34.215 --> 00:11:35.773
when you have full-plate armor,

00:11:35.773 --> 00:11:38.010
you can be run over by a
horse in full-plate armor

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.370
and you'll be just fine.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:40.570
I've seen it happen.

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:43.310
This is kind of a weird
choice in this film,

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:45.230
because El Cid,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:48.660
Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar, is a real person,

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.810
but he lived in the 11th century,

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:53.080
in the late 1000s.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.396
It's largely looking like

00:11:55.396 --> 00:11:58.437
the early- to mid-14th century.

00:11:58.437 --> 00:11:59.682
[crowd cheering]
[armor rustling]

00:11:59.682 --> 00:12:02.470
Uh-oh, the 16th century two-handed swords

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.610
are coming out now.

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.240
We assume for some reason

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:09.603
that a bigger sword hits
harder than a little sword.

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:12.460
These are not firearms.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:15.422
This is not a higher-caliber sword.

00:12:15.422 --> 00:12:16.580
[laughing]

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:20.300
They didn't have two-handed
swords in El Cid's lifetime.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.630
What you're seeing Charlton wearing here

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:26.680
is what's traditionally
referred to as string mail.

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:30.280
It's a knitted garment, knitted
with big knitting needles.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:33.360
So you've got big loops in
the weave of the knitting,

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.030
and then they iron it flat,

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:38.650
spray it silver, and it looks like mail,

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:40.070
or they think it does.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:41.860
It doesn't really look
that much like mail,

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.143
but it's been the mail substitute

00:12:44.143 --> 00:12:45.970
for years and years and years.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:49.490
And it's fine for the background,

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.170
and I'd still pass it for the background,

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:54.270
but you can't have it in the foreground.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.520
Five out of 10.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:06.490
The diving suit is very much like armor

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:08.007
in the sense that it protects you

00:13:08.007 --> 00:13:11.520
from a hostile environment
of a different kind.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.520
It's a complete fantasy,

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.236
because he's not got an air supply.

00:13:16.236 --> 00:13:18.850
Diving suits are a real historical thing.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:20.325
There are designs

00:13:20.325 --> 00:13:23.070
going back to the 14th and 15th centuries

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.253
in the late Middle Ages,

00:13:25.253 --> 00:13:27.361
of designs for diving suits.

00:13:27.361 --> 00:13:29.560
The skills of the armorer

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:32.633
would be recruited

00:13:32.633 --> 00:13:34.480
for doing something like this.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.470
They work purely on a basic
system of water displacement.

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:39.870
The suit is not watertight;

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:40.977
the water can get in.

00:13:40.977 --> 00:13:42.710
You pump air into the helmet,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.430
and it displaces the
water below your face.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:48.955
But the water, as the
pump is going up and down,

00:13:48.955 --> 00:13:50.860
the water goes up like this,

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.710
and it sometimes covers your face,

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:53.810
and sometimes it goes down,

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:55.160
and you hope they pump hard enough

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:56.670
to keep you from drowning.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.060
It's dicey, but it's possible.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:00.570
I give it a seven.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:02.300
It's very, very interesting.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.720
[iron clanging]

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:06.680
Hey, what's he doing?

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:07.756
Stop that!

00:14:07.756 --> 00:14:09.899
[fire hissing]

00:14:09.899 --> 00:14:11.560
No, you can't do that.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.250
That's not how you take
a sword apart, I'm sorry.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.970
This whole idea of
melting a sword blade down

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.370
and then recasting it in a mold,

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:20.770
which is what they're doing,

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:22.700
you can't do that.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:25.375
If you reduce the iron or the steel

00:14:25.375 --> 00:14:27.475
to its liquid state,

00:14:27.475 --> 00:14:31.210
you lose all of the
properties that you want.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:34.470
If you melt and cast iron in a mold,

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:38.750
it's cast iron, and it
becomes really brittle

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:42.240
and you can't temper it and
you can't heat-treat it.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.250
When you have a lump of iron ore

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.880
as it comes out of the ground, yes,

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.990
they heat the iron ore
to separate the iron

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:54.510
from all of the silicates
and the rest of the stuff

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:56.000
that it's mixed up with.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:58.190
You never fully melt it.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:00.950
You don't bring it up to
the full melting point.

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.589
You heat it up until the chemical reaction

00:15:03.589 --> 00:15:06.777
occurs in the ore,
which separates the iron

00:15:06.777 --> 00:15:09.000
from all of the other junk.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:10.800
And it sort of gloops down

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.142
into the bottom of the bloomery.

00:15:13.142 --> 00:15:15.247
Weaponsmiths are very, very careful

00:15:15.247 --> 00:15:17.700
never to fully melt anything.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.870
You cannot melt armor
down and make new armor.

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:25.210
I don't care if it looks
good on screen, it's dumb.

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:26.242
I like some of the things

00:15:26.242 --> 00:15:28.933
that "Game of Thrones" does with armor,

00:15:28.933 --> 00:15:30.508
but that's a fail.

00:15:30.508 --> 00:15:31.667
That's a zero.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.509
I love the sort of evil-Statue-of-Liberty

00:15:36.509 --> 00:15:39.251
sort of aesthetic here.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:47.380
I think the star of
that scene is the flail.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:50.780
Real flails are derived
from farm implements.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:55.210
The articulated staffs that
are used for threshing wheat.

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:57.553
And if you have a feudal peasant army

00:15:57.553 --> 00:16:00.980
and they need to take whatever
hurty things are to hand,

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.320
a flail, you know, agricultural
implements are good.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.700
It's a staff with just
an eyebolt articulation

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:09.550
and then another piece of wood.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:14.370
When such an idea is
weaponized more extensively,

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.832
they add a short length of chain

00:16:16.832 --> 00:16:18.670
between the staff and the head.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:22.770
Flails almost exclusively
are a lower-class,

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.910
a common soldier, infantry weapon.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:26.660
The knights didn't use them.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:29.130
Because they're kind of scary

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:33.830
and they have an inherent
mechanical intrigue,

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:36.053
they've become much more famous

00:16:36.053 --> 00:16:38.936
and much more popular to the kind of

00:16:38.936 --> 00:16:41.661
popular conception of the medieval knight

00:16:41.661 --> 00:16:44.330
than they really deserve to be.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:45.620
But it's great here.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:47.529
I mean, I get it. Totally works here.

00:16:47.529 --> 00:16:49.510
No flail was ever that big.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.080
No flail ever had a chain that long.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:55.550
But in this context it works great.

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.033
I can't think of another weapon

00:16:58.033 --> 00:17:01.107
that would have the kind of visual

00:17:01.107 --> 00:17:05.230
and story, character-driven impact

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.660
that this has in this scene.

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:10.163
It's gotta be a 10 out of 10.

00:17:10.163 --> 00:17:12.238
[man screaming]

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:17.100
He'd be dead.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:18.130
Be very dead.

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:21.473
One man can't attack a
shield wall like that.

00:17:21.473 --> 00:17:23.880
[men screaming]

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.050
Nobody ever used rectangular shields.

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:28.880
I mean, that's ridiculous. It's stupid.

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:30.810
And real early medieval shields,

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:33.680
which were all circular
as far as we can tell,

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.280
if air can get through,

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:37.620
then a lot of other
things can get through.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:39.321
Most real shields of this period

00:17:39.321 --> 00:17:42.604
are made out of boards

00:17:42.604 --> 00:17:45.212
placed edge to edge,

00:17:45.212 --> 00:17:47.330
sort of like this.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:50.030
But they're placed properly edge to edge,

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:53.120
and they're glued, and they're sealed.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:55.540
And there's arguments,

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.048
good arguments to be made for them

00:17:57.048 --> 00:17:59.931
then being edged with rawhide

00:17:59.931 --> 00:18:02.002
or sometimes covered with hide

00:18:02.002 --> 00:18:04.500
or textile and glue.

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:05.880
You should not be able to see

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:07.349
that much air through your shield.

00:18:07.349 --> 00:18:09.705
[men screaming]

00:18:09.705 --> 00:18:10.842
[armor clanging]

00:18:10.842 --> 00:18:11.840
[man shouting in pain]

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.495
He's been stabbed right through the chest

00:18:14.495 --> 00:18:17.530
between those plates.

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:19.950
They're showing you

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.160
how their own

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:25.470
made-up, silly armor doesn't work.

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:27.560
Now, maybe if he gets
hit full in the chest

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.813
with a spear that's wielded by someone

00:18:29.813 --> 00:18:31.220
who knows how to use it,

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:34.970
his padded textile and mail armor,

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:36.240
which is what he should be wearing,

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.840
or even maybe a scale armor,

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.610
maybe it wouldn't stand up either.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:41.057
Now, I'm sorry,

00:18:41.057 --> 00:18:43.130
I can't find anything to hold on to.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:45.609
That's a zero.

00:18:45.609 --> 00:18:46.861
[horse running]

00:18:46.861 --> 00:18:48.605
[man shouting]

00:18:48.605 --> 00:18:50.360
[armor clanging]

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:51.780
Bad lance technique.

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.550
I'll give them a pass on
that, 'cause they wouldn't

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:55.830
have known what good lance technique is.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:56.863
It's a very common thing

00:18:56.863 --> 00:18:59.471
in jousting sequences in movies

00:18:59.471 --> 00:19:02.440
that they lower their lance

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:04.230
to a horizontal,

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:06.640
and then they kick on and gallop along.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:09.990
And they're galloping along
with their lance on the level.

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:11.200
You don't do that.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:12.650
You're sitting on a horse,

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:15.000
you've got 12 feet of pine lance,

00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:16.658
and the horse is going
up and down like this,

00:19:16.658 --> 00:19:17.590
and you've got it here.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.530
It's inevitable that it's
gonna start to do this.

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.590
And it's got a steel head on the end.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.730
There's weight right
out on the end of that.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:26.930
And pretty soon this thing
is going up and down,

00:19:26.930 --> 00:19:30.680
and you couldn't hit a
barn door with the thing.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.840
You've got no chance of controlling

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.040
where that spear goes.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:35.065
And you get the horse going,

00:19:35.065 --> 00:19:37.198
and then you won't have to
worry about your opponent

00:19:37.198 --> 00:19:40.820
until the last second or
so of the interaction.

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:43.070
And then it comes down. Boom.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.320
It takes practice.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:45.850
If you're too late, you miss.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:46.790
If you're too early,

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:49.280
you get tangled up and look like an idiot.

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.530
But if you get it right,

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:52.800
you're always on.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:53.840
A lance came down,

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.620
it got tangled up with
my lance in a joust,

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:58.890
and it came down, and the steel spearhead,

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:01.530
which has three prongs like this,

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:04.000
two of the prongs went past my hand

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:06.590
and the third one bypassed the defenses

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:08.330
of my armor, the gauntlet.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:11.060
It went just under the plate,

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:13.340
through the leather glove and into me.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:15.130
I finished the competition

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:18.606
before I even realized I
needed to go get sewn up.

00:20:18.606 --> 00:20:20.510
[armor clanging]

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:22.770
Lancelot: Yield, sir!
I have the advantage.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.330
Toby: Now, again, this is all metal armor.

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:26.450
It's all aluminum,

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:29.910
aluminum sprayed black
or aluminum polished,

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.040
but look how good it looks.

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.190
They worked hard to do
what they were doing,

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:34.530
and they knew what they were doing,

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.622
and I think it deserves a seven.

00:20:38.176 --> 00:20:41.240
[armor clanging]

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:42.749
Traditional ranged weapons,

00:20:42.749 --> 00:20:45.440
like the longbow and the crossbow,

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:49.474
the projectile isn't moving that fast,

00:20:49.474 --> 00:20:52.113
but it's still moving way fast enough.

00:20:53.797 --> 00:20:55.110
You can't block arrows.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:58.450
Knight: Remove the hood and the mask.

00:20:59.454 --> 00:21:00.890
[dramatic music]

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.557
[Toby laughing]

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:07.647
Toby: It's an interesting
outfit he's got on.

00:21:07.647 --> 00:21:09.350
You know, who has he come as?

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:11.530
He's got a vaguely 11th,

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:13.890
early-12th-century helmet,

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:16.250
but he's got full-plate armor under there

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:18.423
like the 16th century or something.

00:21:19.403 --> 00:21:20.386
I'd give it a two

00:21:20.386 --> 00:21:22.768
because it's funny and it's Nicolas Cage.

00:21:22.768 --> 00:21:24.020
[producer laughing]

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:25.623
Oh, it's funny.

00:21:25.623 --> 00:21:28.030
[producer laughing]

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:29.580
I guess that's worth something.

